<section id="limits" class="section">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <h2>Where execution breaks down</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        The outcome distribution shows how much task score gains at termination. The recordings
        help explain the underlying difficulties: precise contact and completing a sequence despite
        local failures.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="report-prose">
      <p>
        Across 510 episodes, 237 reach complete success. Of the 273 incomplete episodes, 188
        receive positive partial credit and 85 score zero. Most incomplete episodes retain
        positive task credit. The task-level breakdown distinguishes zero terminal credit from
        partial completion; the videos then show how grasp loss, contact errors and unfinished
        procedures lead to these outcomes.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div id="episode-outcomes"></div>
    <div class="outcome-task-breakdown" id="failure-depth-analysis">
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Zero-score episodes account for 75% of gear installation, 65% of utensils placement and
          50% of glasses packing. In contrast, nut tightening, peg insertion and shop have no
          zero-score episodes; bottle has 30%. Their low success rates mask different outcome
          distributions.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Zero-score episodes receive no credit under the task’s scoring rules.</strong> The
          videos and interaction logs show where execution stops.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="failure-depth-chart"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="report-prose">
      <p>
        These terminal outcomes motivate examining where precise contact fails and how local
        retries affect completion of a longer procedure. The following recordings connect those
        execution difficulties to the task results.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="limit-switch" aria-label="Execution challenge">
      <button data-limit="precision" aria-pressed="true">
        Precise contact
      </button>
      <button data-limit="horizon" aria-pressed="false">
        Long procedures
      </button>
    </div>
    <div id="limits-content"></div>
    <div class="insight-block" id="fixed-base-analysis">
      <h3>The tabletop gap extends beyond high precision</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          On the three tabletop tasks labelled low or medium precision—frame placement, cup flipping
          and glasses packing—GPT-6-Astra averages 31.67% success, compared with Qwen-RobotManip’s
          91.67%. Cup flipping reaches 10% versus 90%; glasses packing reaches 20% versus 90%.
          Removing the high-precision tasks therefore does not remove the tabletop deficit.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Precision labels alone do not explain the capability gap.</strong> Depth and
          contact estimation, object orientation and coordination between the arms are plausible
          contributors, consistent with the qualitative trajectory analysis. The glasses comparison
          shows an unresolved folding and closure sequence; the frame ICL case illustrates how a
          demonstration can supply a better division of labour between the arms.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="fixed-task-evidence"></div>
    </div>
